;;; `open` bound to a relation parameter, beside a fact of `open`, loads and is
;;; read as a relation — M1f **W70**, register row `W70/relation-parameter`,
;;; filed by S1f.5.23 (T1f.5.23.2, 2026-11-10).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `copy`'s `?R` is a relation parameter; the activator `(copy open)` binds it
;;; to `open`, and `(open k)` is a fact of that relation, so `copy` reads it.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `open` is the verdict a rule's `:assert` states, `(open)` or `(open ?R)`,
;;; and is written nowhere else (the user's ruling on Q-M1f.66, 2026-11-10):
;;; here it is written as an activator's argument bound to a relation parameter,
;;; and a fact's relation, so the program is refused at the parse, at the
;;; `open`, and every command exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `f62e34e1`, release: the program loads, `copy` reads `(open k)` and root
;;; derives `(q k)`; R is 2 states, exit 0 on every command.
;;; Refused at the parse since S1f.5.23 (T1f.5.23.3).

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(rule copy (?R)
  :match  (?R ?x)
  :assert (q ?x)
  :why "{?x} is in {?R}, so it is a q")
(copy open)
(open k)
